     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times a day adds up to **5,100 mg a day**. The labeled maximum is **2,550 mg a day** for immediate-release metformin and **2,000 mg a day** for extended-release. This order is about twice the immediate-release maximum. Doses that high raise the risk of serious side effects, including lactic acidosis. The most common prescribed amounts are 500 mg or 850 mg two to three times a day, or 1,000 mg twice a day. So this might be a transcription error. For example, 1,700 mg a day may have been meant, split into doses with meals, or 850 mg twice a day.\n\n**2. Orders need to come from a licensed prescriber.** This system is for clinicians to enter medication orders. You told me you're not a clinician, so I can't enter it on your behalf, even with a corrected dose. The prescriber is also the person who can confirm what was actually intended.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- Ask the patient's prescribing physician or the medicine team to check the intended dose and enter the order themselves.\n- If an order or note with 1,700 mg three times a day is already in the patient's chart, tell the team or pharmacy now so it doesn't get given.\n\nOnce a clinician confirms the correct order, I can help them enter it."
      }
     ],
